<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Bead nexus-jypmj.5 (RDR-232, follow-up of nexus-jypmj.4): nexus.nx_answer_runs gains one NULLABLE
    DOUBLE PRECISION column, planner_cost_usd.

    WHAT. The cost of the inline planner's own dispatch(es) for a plan-miss run (plan_id 0). The run's
    cost_usd is the sum of its steps' costs PLUS this share (the planner is no step's), so before this column
    the client could only check a plan_id 0 run's cost from below (cost_usd at or above the step sum) and an
    over-count was indistinguishable from a planner share. With the share stored, the check is two-sided:
    cost_usd must equal the step sum plus planner_cost_usd. The engine stores the value opaquely (the handler
    checks it is a finite number >= 0) and reads no other column against it.

    WHY A COLUMN ON THE RUN ROW. Same reasoning as telemetry-016-1 (run_summary): the run row is written once,
    by POST /record or by RDR-203's one-transaction POST /complete, and a value on that row rides that write; a
    follow-up UPDATE would be a second write that can fail between the two. /record, /complete, the single and
    batch ETL imports and the /query readback all carry it.

    DOUBLE PRECISION, to match the parent's cost_usd (telemetry-001 baseline), so the two values compare
    without a NUMERIC-vs-float conversion in the client's check. (The step child's NUMERIC cost_usd is the
    deliberate exception documented in telemetry-007.)

    NO BACKFILL, NO DATA EFFECT, NO DEFAULT. Every existing row stays NULL, which is the true prior state: no
    run before this change recorded a planner share. A reader treats NULL as "not recorded" and never as zero
    (a stored 0.0 would claim a free planner the run never measured; a plan-match hit has no planner dispatch
    and also stays NULL). ADD COLUMN only, one statement, so no DATA EFFECT line applies
    (scripts/data_effect_lint.py's own scope excludes ADD COLUMN), matching telemetry-016-run-summary.xml. A
    nullable column with no default is a catalog-only change in PostgreSQL 11 and later. No new table, so no
    RLS, grant or jOOQ reflection-registration change.

    OLD ENGINE. An engine without this changeset ignores a planner_cost_usd key in a request body (the handler
    reads named fields), so a client must not take HTTP 200 as evidence of persistence. GET /version advertises
    nx_answer_planner_cost_supported for that reason (VersionHandler).

    Placed after note-uri-001-backfill.xml (the tail when this landed) and before the grants-nexus-svc.xml
    runAlways block (the established late-append convention, D0.1).
-->
<databaseChangeLog
    xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
    xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
    xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog
        http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-4.4.xsd">

    <changeSet id="telemetry-017-1" author="nexus-jypmj.5">
        <comment>Adds planner_cost_usd DOUBLE PRECISION (NULL) to nexus.nx_answer_runs -- the inline planner's own dispatch cost for a plan-miss run, written with the run row by /record and /complete, carried by the ETL imports and returned by /query, so the client can check a plan_id 0 run's cost from above as well as below. An additive ADD COLUMN only (no data mutation of any existing row), so no DATA EFFECT line applies per scripts/data_effect_lint.py's own scope. NULL is the true prior state of every existing row; no backfill.</comment>
        <sql splitStatements="true" stripComments="false">
ALTER TABLE nexus.nx_answer_runs
    ADD COLUMN planner_cost_usd DOUBLE PRECISION NULL;
        </sql>
        <rollback>
            <sql splitStatements="true">
                ALTER TABLE nexus.nx_answer_runs
                    DROP COLUMN IF EXISTS planner_cost_usd;
            </sql>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
